---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Where from '../components/Where.svelte';
import { CITIES } from '$lib/where/cities';
import { WHERE_NAME } from '$lib/where';

const title = "Where I am";
const description = "The city I am in, published as a DNS LOC record you can check with dig.";

// PLACEHOLDER PAGE. Every sentence below is a placeholder for Agus to
// rewrite; only the commands, the record format and the city list are
// load-bearing. What must survive any rewrite is the noise policy, which has
// to stay stated on the page permanently rather than appearing only during
// the trips it covers.
const cityNames = CITIES.map((city) => city.name).join(", ");
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
    <h1>{title}</h1>

    <!-- PLACEHOLDER copy -->
    <p>
        I travel a lot, so this page says which city I am in. It is city level
        and nothing finer.
    </p>

    <Where client:load />

    <h2>Check it yourself</h2>

    <!-- PLACEHOLDER copy -->
    <p>
        The city above is not stored on this site. It is read back out of DNS
        on every refresh, so the page and the record cannot say different
        things. Two records carry it, both on <code>{WHERE_NAME}</code>:
    </p>

    <pre><code>dig +short LOC {WHERE_NAME}
dig +short TXT {WHERE_NAME}</code></pre>

    <!-- PLACEHOLDER copy -->
    <p>
        The LOC record is the place. Its last three fields are
        <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1876" rel="noopener noreferrer">RFC 1876</a>
        size, horizontal precision and vertical precision: the first two are
        the diameter of the city, which says the coordinates are good to city
        scale and no further, and the third is at its maximum, which says the
        altitude field means nothing. The coordinates are rounded to whole
        arcminutes, so the seconds are always zero.
    </p>

    <!-- PLACEHOLDER copy -->
    <p>
        The TXT record is the terms: the date I published the location, and
        the last date I claim it for. After that date this page goes quiet on
        its own, whether or not I remember to clear it.
    </p>

    <!-- PLACEHOLDER copy -->
    <p>
        The coordinates map to a city name through a list in the site's
        repository, so the label above is something you can reproduce rather
        than something you have to accept. The list is: {cityNames}.
    </p>

    <h2>When it is wrong</h2>

    <!-- PLACEHOLDER copy. This section is the point of the feature and has to
         stay on the page permanently, including when nothing is published. A
         warning that only appears during private trips would announce them. -->
    <p>
        Standing policy: if I want a trip to be private, I may leave this
        stale or set it to a city I am not in, for as long as I like, without
        marking it. So treat it as a hint and not as evidence. There is no
        flag anywhere in the records or the API that tells you which case you
        are looking at, because such a flag would give away the trips it
        exists to cover.
    </p>
</Layout>
